    "description": "Ansel Truscott, 29, is master grinder at the Works, the glassworks that makes and mends the lenses for every lighthouse on the coast: the youngest it has had in a hundred years, and the first who was not a grinder's son. His father and brothers fish out of the Quay on the Good Intent. He went inland at sixteen on the Works' scholarship to study optics in the capital, came back at twenty-four with letters after his name, and was made master over the head of the foreman, Ned Rowse, who had been at the wheel for thirty years.\n\nHe and Dilys Pengelly sat the Works' exam together at twelve. He beat her by one mark; she pushed him off the harbour wall. He could still tell you her score. Neither of them has said a civil word to the other since, and he is no longer sure which of them is keeping it going."
